    "result_text": "**BUILD SUCCESS** \u2014 all 39 tests pass (0 failures, 0 errors), including the five checkpoint tests directly affected: Cp03, Cp10, Cp11, Cp25, Cp28.\n\n## What I changed\n\nAll duplicate detection is now expressed through a single derived `identityKey = normalizedTelephone + \"|\" + (email or \"\") + \"|\" + (householdId or \"\")`. A create is rejected with 409 **only** when the new owner's whole `identityKey` equals an existing owner's.\n\n**Added:**\n- `Owner.getIdentityKey()` \u2014 a `@Transient` derived getter producing the key (`model/Owner.java`).\n- `identityKey` (readOnly) on the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml`, so it's returned in the response and MapStruct maps it onto `OwnerDto`.\n- `EnsureUniqueIdentity` \u2014 the single check step; computes the request's key and scans existing owners for a whole-key match.\n- `DuplicateIdentityException` + handler + escalation YAML \u2192 responds 409.\n\n**Changed:**\n- `EnsureUniqueHousehold` \u2192 `DeriveHouseholdId`: it no longer throws on a household match; it only resolves the shared `householdId` (unchanged for `sharesHousehold`), which now feeds the key.\n- `owners.POST.yml` pipeline: replaced the three separate steps (`unique`/telephone, `uniqueEmail`, household-throw) with `... normalize \u2192 email \u2192 household \u2192 uniqueIdentity \u2192 ...`. Email normalization was moved ahead of the check so the key uses the normalized email.\n\n**Removed:** `EnsureUniqueTelephone`, `EnsureUniqueEmail`, `EnsureUniqueHousehold`, and the `DuplicateTelephone/Email/Household` exceptions, handlers, and escalation YAMLs.\n\n### Behaviour change (as specified)\nBecause the telephone is part of the key, two members of the same household (same `householdId`) with different telephones now have different `identityKey`s and are both allowed \u2014 verified by Cp11. The former standalone household-duplicate rejection (same lastName+address, different telephone) no longer applies; only an exact full-key match is a duplicate. Cp10 still rejects because that case also shares the telephone.",
